@startuml AWS_Federation_With_OnPrem_AD
title AWS Resource Access via Federation with On-Premises AD Trust

actor "User\n(On-Prem Employee)" as User
participant "Browser /\nAWS CLI" as Client
participant "On-Premises\nActive Directory" as OnPremAD
participant "AD FS\n(Identity Provider)" as ADFS
participant "AWS Managed\nMicrosoft AD" as ManagedAD
participant "AWS STS\n(Security Token Service)" as STS
participant "AWS IAM" as IAM
participant "AWS Resource\n(e.g., S3, EC2)" as Resource

== Authentication Phase ==
User -> Client: 1. Access AWS Console / Service
Client -> ADFS: 2. Redirect to Identity Provider
ADFS -> User: 3. Prompt for corporate credentials
User -> ADFS: 4. Submit username/password
ADFS -> OnPremAD: 5. Validate credentials (Kerberos/LDAP)
OnPremAD -> ADFS: 6. Authentication success + user attributes

note over OnPremAD, ManagedAD
  Trust relationship exists between
  On-Prem AD and AWS Managed AD
  (via VPN or Direct Connect)
end note

== Token Generation Phase ==
ADFS -> ADFS: 7. Generate SAML assertion\n(signed with IdP cert)
ADFS -> Client: 8. Return SAML token\n(with role mappings)

== AWS Authorization Phase ==
Client -> STS: 9. AssumeRoleWithSAML\n(SAML assertion + Role ARN)
STS -> IAM: 10. Validate SAML trust\n& check role policy
IAM -> STS: 11. Role validation success
STS -> STS: 12. Generate temporary\ncredentials (1hr default)
STS -> Client: 13. Return temp credentials\n(AccessKey, SecretKey, SessionToken)

== Resource Access Phase ==
Client -> Resource: 14. API call with temp credentials
Resource -> IAM: 15. Verify permissions
IAM -> Resource: 16. Authorization decision
Resource -> Client: 17. Return requested data
Client -> User: 18. Display result

note over User, Resource
  No passwords stored in AWS.
  Credentials expire automatically.
  Access revoked when user disabled in On-Prem AD.
end note

@enduml
